needs Mesa's `libgbm`, `libEGL` and `libGLESv2` for the host, which this box does not carry

`tools/test-all.sh` reads the first line of this file and reports it as the
reason for the skip.

This probe exists to prove one thing on one machine: that a Rivet card's VC4
brings up GBM and EGL and that a GLES program draws through it (`PLAN.md`
§104.15). The libraries it links come from Buildroot for aarch64 and are on the
card; the host copies would be Arch's `mesa`, which drags LLVM behind it — a
hundred megabytes and a build-time cost, in the box every other check runs in,
so that a program which cannot run without a GPU can be *linked* here.

It is worth saying what this skip does NOT excuse. `tools/card/build.sh` builds
this for the target and `cargo rivet package` signs it, so it is compiled by the
loop that matters; what is skipped is only the host build. ~~And the audit of
2026-09-03 has open findings against this probe — it ignores `RIVET_DISPLAY` and
writes `/dev/fb0` while the launcher holds the screen — which are behaviour, not
compilation, and are not made any less open by this file.~~

**The finding was closed in the source on 2026-09-04** (register `R-33`):
`allowed_to_draw` decides from `RIVET_APP_DATA` and `RIVET_DISPLAY` whether
anything else could be holding the panel, and the framebuffer is not opened at
all in the two cases where it could be. The struck sentence is kept because it
is what a skip file is for — saying out loud what the skip is not covering.

**And the skip still bites, in a new place.** `allowed_to_draw` has a test, and
that test **has never been executed.** The crate cannot link for the host, so
`cargo test` cannot build it; the container has no aarch64 interpreter, so the
target test binary cannot be run. What was verified is: `cargo build --release
--target aarch64-unknown-linux-musl` succeeds against Buildroot's Mesa with the
same seven warnings it had before the change, and `cargo test --no-run` for that
target compiles the test binary. **A test that has never run proves nothing**,
so the behaviour above is unproven until this probe runs on a board — from the
launcher's menu, and beside it.
